  "rationale": "The requirement's only structural obligation is the signature `impl Sources { pub async fn list(&self, scope: &SourceScope) -> Result<Vec<PathBuf>, SourceError> }`. `crates/trust-but-verify/src/sources/public.rs#impl[Sources]/fn[list]` declares exactly that: a `pub async fn` on `impl Sources` that takes `&self` and `&SourceScope` and returns `Result<Vec<PathBuf>, SourceError>`.\n\nThe body delegates to `self.analysis.provider.list(scope)`, which is the provider interface `crates/trust-but-verify/src/sources.rs#trait[SourceProvider]/fn[list]`. That method returns `Vec<ResourceId>`, and the body maps each id to `PathBuf::from(id.location)`.\n\n`Sources.analysis` is `SourceAnalysis<LocalSources>`, and `LocalSources` sets `type Error = SourceError` in its `SourceProvider` impl. So the `?` in the body yields `SourceError` directly. The trait also bounds `Error: From<SourceError>`.\n\n`SourceProvider::list` carries the matching `{impl}` marker, and the public `Sources::list` is the root marked for the requirement. I found no structural gaps. The requirement text states no behavioral obligations beyond the signature and its doc comment.",
